    "summary": "High‑energy UJB > Samson > UJB sandwich, sweet Althea, booming Wheel",
    "blurb": "The September 9 ‘82 Saenger show bursts with a killer Uncle John's Band → Samson → Uncle John's Band sandwich and a gorgeous Althea that carries the band through an uneven first set. The second set lifts the night with a powerful Wheel into a rousing encore, while Brent’s keyboards add texture throughout.",
    "review": "What unfolds at Saenger Performing Arts Center is a quintessential early‑’80s Dead outing: a rough start in the first set—Bob Weir leaves stage early during Althea—but Jerry Garcia’s sweet, soaring tone on that very song rescues the momentum. The real highlight arrives in the second set, where the band launches into an inspired Uncle John's Band → Samson & Delilah → Uncle John's Band sandwich; Brent Mydland’s organ swells under the jam and Phil Lesh roars like a beast on bass, driving the group forward. From there the set rides a strong Estimated Prophet into an expansive Eyes Of The World, then erupts with a thunderous Wheel that segues into The Other One, Wharf Rat and a jubilant Good Lovin’ before the encore’s Keep Your Day Job/Johnny B. Goode finale. Despite a few lyrical slips and equipment hiccups, the performance remains high‑energy and tightly locked in, making it one of the more memorable shows of the 1982 tour.",
